<% content_for(:title, "ForgeCI — CI/CD that speaks CircleCI") %>

<!-- ================= HERO ================= -->
<section style="padding-top: 88px;">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="hero-grid">
      <div>
        <span class="eyebrow">Open source &middot; MIT licensed</span>
        <h1 class="hero-title">CI/CD that speaks <span class="accent">CircleCI</span></h1>
        <p class="hero-sub">
          ForgeCI runs your <code>.circleci/config.yml</code>-style pipelines on your own
          infrastructure. A pure-Ruby compiler turns CircleCI&nbsp;2.1 configs into
          pipelines; Docker runners execute them and stream the logs back live.
        </p>
        <div class="cta-row">
          <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/projects">Open dashboard</a>
          <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="https://github.com/azmi2409/forge-ci">View on GitHub</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="terminal">
        <div class="terminal-bar">
          <span class="dot r"></span><span class="dot y"></span><span class="dot g"></span>
          <span class="terminal-title">.forge-ci/config.yml</span>
        </div>
<pre class="terminal-body"><code><span class="c"># Looks like CircleCI. That's the point.</span>
<span class="k">version</span>: <span class="s">2.1</span>

<span class="k">jobs</span>:
  <span class="k">build</span>:
    <span class="k">docker</span>:
      - <span class="k">image</span>: <span class="s">cimg/ruby:3.4</span>
    <span class="k">steps</span>:
      - <span class="k">checkout</span>
      - <span class="k">run</span>:
          <span class="k">name</span>: <span class="s">Install dependencies</span>
          <span class="k">command</span>: <span class="s">bundle install</span>
      - <span class="k">run</span>:
          <span class="k">name</span>: <span class="s">Run tests</span>
          <span class="k">command</span>: <span class="s">bundle exec rake test</span>

<span class="k">workflows</span>:
  <span class="k">build-and-test</span>:
    <span class="k">jobs</span>:
      - <span class="k">build</span></code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= FEATURES ================= -->
<section id="features">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2 class="section-title">Everything a CI needs. Nothing it doesn't.</h2>
    <p class="section-sub">A complete continuous-integration system in two pieces: a Rails control plane and zero-dependency Docker runners.</p>
    <div class="features">
      <div class="feature">
        <div class="icon">&#128260;</div>
        <h3>CircleCI 2.1 compatible</h3>
        <p>A pure-Ruby, zero-dependency config compiler turns CircleCI-style YAML into executable pipelines. Bring your existing config and muscle memory.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature">
        <div class="icon">&#128051;</div>
        <h3>Docker runners</h3>
        <p>Stdlib-only Ruby runners claim queued jobs and run each step in isolated Docker containers. Scale horizontally — just add more runners.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature">
        <div class="icon">&#128225;</div>
        <h3>Live log streaming</h3>
        <p>Watch job output in real time. Runners push log chunks over the API; the dashboard tails them via Server-Sent Events.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature">
        <div class="icon">&#128190;</div>
        <h3>Cache &amp; artifacts</h3>
        <p>Dependency caches and build artifacts backed by S3, with per-project scoping and configurable retention.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature">
        <div class="icon">&#128272;</div>
        <h3>Contexts &amp; secrets</h3>
        <p>Encrypted environment contexts shared across projects, injected at runtime — with admin-gated secret rotation.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature">
        <div class="icon">&#128025;</div>
        <h3>GitHub App integration</h3>
        <p>Push webhooks trigger pipelines automatically, and commit statuses report build results back to your pull requests.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= HOW IT WORKS ================= -->
<section id="how-it-works">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2 class="section-title">How it works</h2>
    <p class="section-sub">Three stages, from <code>git push</code> to green build.</p>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <h3>Push</h3>
        <p>Install the GitHub App on your repos. Every push fires a webhook; ForgeCI fetches <code>.forge-ci/config.yml</code> at that commit.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <h3>Compile</h3>
        <p>The control plane compiles your CircleCI-style config into a pipeline of jobs, workflows, and dependencies — validated before anything runs.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <h3>Run</h3>
        <p>Runners claim queued jobs, execute each step in Docker, and stream logs live to the dashboard while commit statuses update on GitHub.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= ARCHITECTURE ================= -->
<section id="architecture">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2 class="section-title">Simple architecture</h2>
    <p class="section-sub">One control plane, as many runners as you need, boring dependable storage.</p>
    <div class="arch">
<pre>                    ┌─────────────────────┐
                    │     <span class="hl">GitHub App</span>        │
                    │  webhooks + statuses │
                    └──────────┬──────────┘
                               │ push
                    ┌──────────▼──────────┐      ┌──────────────┐
                    │   <span class="hl">Control plane</span>     │─────▶│   <span class="hl">Postgres</span>   │
                    │      (Rails 8)      │      │  jobs + state│
                    └──────────┬──────────┘      └──────────────┘
                               │ claim / heartbeat / logs
                    ┌──────────▼──────────┐      ┌──────────────┐
                    │ <span class="hl">Runners</span> (Docker)    │─────▶│      <span class="hl">S3</span>      │
                    │   zero-dep Ruby     │      │ logs/artifacts│
                    └─────────────────────┘      └──────────────┘</pre>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= QUICKSTART ================= -->
<section id="quickstart">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2 class="section-title">Quickstart</h2>
    <p class="section-sub">The whole stack — Postgres, control plane, worker, runner — with one compose file.</p>
    <div class="terminal">
      <div class="terminal-bar">
        <span class="dot r"></span><span class="dot y"></span><span class="dot g"></span>
        <span class="terminal-title">terminal</span>
      </div>
<pre class="terminal-body"><code><span class="p">$</span> cp .env.example .env   <span class="c"># fill in S3 credentials + bucket, pick a RUNNER_TOKEN secret</span>
<span class="p">$</span> openssl rand -hex 32   <span class="c"># → paste into .env as RUNNER_TOKEN</span>
<span class="p">$</span> docker compose up --build -d
<span class="p">$</span> docker compose up -d runner
<span class="p">$</span> <span class="c"># → open http://localhost:3000</span></code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="qs-note">
      Object storage is AWS S3 — create the bucket first
      (<code>aws s3 mb s3://&lt;your-bucket&gt;</code>). The same
      <code>RUNNER_TOKEN</code> value authenticates the runner against the control
      plane, so there's no token to copy around. See
      <a href="https://github.com/azmi2409/forge-ci/blob/main/DEPLOY.md">DEPLOY.md</a>
      for production deploys.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
